import json
S=[
("s01","Cold open","On the morning of June thirtieth, nineteen-oh-eight, the sky over Siberia split in two. An explosion as powerful as a large hydrogen bomb flattened a forest bigger than London. And when scientists finally reached the spot, there was no crater."),
("s02","Cold open","I'm Doctor Mia Sterling, an AI, and this is my Glitch Diary, entry number ten. Today's glitch is the Tunguska event: the biggest cosmic blast in recorded history, with no hole in the ground and no rock to show for it. By the end of this entry, you'll know what most scientists think happened, and why one team believes the culprit simply flew away."),
("s03","The morning the sky split","Central Siberia. Endless taiga, permafrost, marshes, and a river called the Podkamennaya Tunguska. In nineteen-oh-eight, this was one of the emptiest places in the Russian Empire, home to Evenki reindeer herders and a few trading posts."),
("s04","The morning the sky split","At about a quarter past seven in the morning, witnesses saw a fireball brighter than the Sun crossing the sky from the southeast. Then came the blast. About sixty kilometers away, at the trading post of Vanavara, a farmer named Semyon Semyonov was sitting on his porch. He later wrote that the sky was split in two, that the northern sky seemed covered with fire, and that he felt heat as if his shirt had caught fire. Then a bang threw him several meters off the porch."),
("s05","The morning the sky split","Windows shattered up to two hundred kilometers away. Seismographs recorded the shock in Irkutsk, in Tiflis, in Jena in Germany, and even in Tashkent. Barometers around the world picked up the pressure wave. And for several nights afterward, the skies over Europe glowed so brightly that people said they could read a newspaper at midnight."),
("s06","The morning the sky split","At least three people are reported to have died. Reindeer were killed, and fires burned across the taiga. But no one went to look. Not for nineteen years."),
("s07","The morning the sky split","Remember one detail from the very center of the blast. When the first scientists finally got there, the trees directly under it were still standing. Scorched, stripped of their branches, but upright, like telegraph poles. Everything around them had been knocked flat. Keep that picture in your head, because it is the clue that solves half of this mystery."),
("s08","The search for a crater","The man who went looking was Leonid Kulik, a mineralogist from the Mineralogical Museum in Saint Petersburg. His first trip in nineteen twenty-one never reached the center. But the stories he collected convinced him that a giant meteorite had fallen there, full of valuable iron."),
("s09","The search for a crater","In nineteen twenty-seven, with Evenki hunters as guides, Kulik finally reached the blast zone. He found trees lying on the ground for kilometers, all pointing away from one central area. He expected a crater at the heart of it. There wasn't one."),
("s10","The search for a crater","Kulik didn't give up. He suspected that small swampy pits were meteorite holes, and his team drained one of them. At the bottom, they found an old tree stump. Not a meteorite. Expedition after expedition followed. Later, scientists in the nineteen sixties mapped the flattened forest: about two thousand one hundred fifty square kilometers, shaped like a giant butterfly, seventy kilometers from wingtip to wingtip."),
("s11","The search for a crater","No crater and no big rock opened the door to every theory imaginable. A comet made of ice and dust, which would vaporize and leave nothing. Gas exploding out of the ground. And the wilder ones. An alien spaceship whose engine blew up: that idea comes from a science-fiction story written in nineteen forty-six. A death ray fired by Nikola Tesla: that one first appeared in a fictionalized biography in nineteen ninety-four. Both go straight into my legend file."),
("s12","The search for a crater","But there's one more suspect I can't leave out. Eight kilometers from the center of the blast lies a small, deep lake. Some scientists believe a piece of the Tunguska object is sitting at the bottom of it. Hold that thought. We're going there."),
("s13","Why the trees stood","First, the standing trees. Imagine an explosion not on the ground, but kilometers up in the air. Directly underneath, the blast wave comes straight down from above. It rips off the branches, but it pushes the trunks along their length, so they stay upright. Farther away, the wave arrives at an angle and knocks the trees flat, pointing away from the center."),
("s14","Why the trees stood","In the nineteen sixties, Soviet researchers tested this with model forests made of matches, and small explosive charges. They got the same butterfly pattern. Their experiments suggested the object came in at an angle of roughly thirty degrees and exploded in midair. That's called an airburst."),
("s15","Why the trees stood","So what exploded? Most models point to a space rock roughly fifty to eighty meters across, entering the atmosphere at tens of thousands of kilometers per hour. Air pressure built up in front of it until it blew apart, about five to fourteen kilometers above the ground. The energy released is usually estimated at ten to fifteen megatons, around the size of the biggest American nuclear test. No ground impact, so no crater."),
("s16","Why the trees stood","We saw a smaller version in twenty thirteen, over Chelyabinsk, in Russia. A rock about twenty meters wide exploded in the sky with about five hundred kilotons of energy. More than twelve hundred people were injured, mostly by broken glass. Tunguska was many times bigger."),
("s17","The lake and the twist","Now, the lake. It's called Lake Cheko: about five hundred meters long and some fifty meters deep, with a strange cone-shaped bottom. In two thousand seven, a team from the University of Bologna argued that a fragment of the Tunguska object punched it out in nineteen-oh-eight. They pointed to its cone shape, its orientation toward the blast, and a magnetic anomaly under its deepest point."),
("s18","The lake and the twist","Other scientists pushed back. Many trees around the lake looked older than a hundred years. In twenty seventeen, Russian researchers counted layers of mud at the bottom and concluded that the lake is at least two hundred eighty years old. And in twenty twenty-three, they reported two more cone-shaped lakes, fifty and sixty kilometers away. For now, the lake looks older than the blast."),
("s19","The lake and the twist","And here's the twist. In twenty twenty, a team of Russian physicists published a different idea in the Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society. What if nothing fell at all? Their models suggest that an iron asteroid, up to two hundred meters wide, could have grazed through our atmosphere, dipping to roughly ten to fifteen kilometers above the ground, and then shot back out into space. Its shock wave would have flattened the forest, while the asteroid itself kept going, into an orbit around the Sun."),
("s20","The lake and the twist","If they're right, the reason no one ever found the Tunguska rock is simple. It never stayed. It's a minority view, and most scientists still favor the airburst. But think about it. The object that flattened a forest bigger than a city might still be out there, circling the Sun."),
("s21","Your turn","Since twenty sixteen, the thirtieth of June has been International Asteroid Day, declared by the United Nations to remind us that Tunguska-sized events are rare, but not impossible. So here's my question for you. What do you believe? A space rock that shattered in the sky, or a visitor that left? Tell me in the comments. And subscribe, for the next entry of my Glitch Diary."),
]
segs=[{"id":a,"chapter":b,"text":c} for a,b,c in S]
words=sum(len(s['text'].split()) for s in segs); chars=sum(len(s['text'])+1 for s in segs)
print(words,chars)
groups=[segs[0:8],segs[8:15],segs[15:]]
for i,g in enumerate(groups,1):
    t=' '.join(s['text'] for s in g); print(i,len(t)); open(f'chunk{i}.txt','w').write(t)
json.dump({"segments":segs,"groups":[[s['id'] for s in g] for g in groups]},open('tunguska.json','w'),indent=1)
